00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:20,350
of space. And so where does that space get allocated? How do we make sure it gets

51
00:05:20,350 --> 00:05:27,810
allocated properly? In particular, in C, as you know, when a function returns, any local data

52
00:05:27,810 --> 00:05:33,850
that it allocated should get deallocated, should be freed up so that we don't sort of start

53
00:05:33,850 --> 00:05:39,670
consuming an unbounded amount of storage. So how do we do that? Those are all the sort of

54
00:05:39,670 --> 00:05:45,710
different aspects that a procedure call and return have to deal with somehow. And

55
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:50,770
And part of the reason for breaking it down into those little segments is in

56
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:58,290
X8664, one of the clever things they did was to try and reduce the overhead of procedure calls

57
00:05:58,290 --> 00:06:05,730
as much as possible because, as you know, in good programming style, you often write these functions

58
00:06:05,730 --> 00:06:10,130
that do a fairly small amount of actual useful stuff.

