00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:42,100
stack in various ways. The stack is really not a special memory. It's just a region

74
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:47,380
of the normal memory. Remember, to the programmer's perspective,

75
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:53,940
assembly level programmers perspective is memory is just a big array of bytes.

76
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:59,060
And somewhere within that bunch of bytes, we're going to call it the stack.

77
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:05,500
And the stack is used by the program to manage the state associated with the procedures that it

78
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:14,060
calls as they return. So it's where it passes all these potential information, the control information,

79
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:20,820
information, data, and allocates local data. And the reason why it can be managed

80
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:27,200
is a stack is because of the nature of the whole idea of procedure calls and returns, that you make

81
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:34,020
a call and you might need some information, but when you return from a call, all that information

82
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:40,750
can be discarded. And so it makes use of that sort of last in, in, first out, allocation

83
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:46,340
principle meshes very well with this idea of procedure call and return.

